reproduction.explanation:
The reported buffer overflow at lib/ws.c:581 does not exist. The code has an explicit bounds check at lines 578-580 that prevents the strcpy from overflowing:

```c
DEBUGASSERT(randlen < sizeof(keyval));
if(randlen >= sizeof(keyval))
  return CURLE_FAILED_INIT;
strcpy(keyval, randstr);
```

The `keyval` buffer is 40 bytes (line 541). The nonce is generated locally by `Curl_rand` (16 random bytes, line 572) and base64-encoded by `Curl_base64_encode` (line 575). Base64 encoding of 16 bytes produces exactly 24 characters (plus NUL = 25), well within the 40-byte buffer. The bounds check ensures `strcpy` is only reached when `randlen < sizeof(keyval)`.

The reporter mischaracterizes this as accepting attacker-controlled input. In reality, this is the WebSocket *client* code (Curl_ws_request) generating the Sec-WebSocket-Key header per RFC 6455 - the nonce is locally generated, not supplied by a server or attacker. No network-facing test can trigger an overflow because the input to strcpy is entirely locally generated and validated.

